## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` conda
environment (pandas 3.0.5, numpy 2.5.2, pydeseq2 0.5.4, scikit-learn 1.9.0, matplotlib 3.11.1,
per `_references/gars-bio.lock.txt`), submitted as a single batch job.

1. **Resolve inputs** with `resolve_artifact.py --consumes counts_gene design` (above); abort on
   any `problems`.
2. **Load counts.** Read the `counts_gene` TSV; index by `gene_id`, keep `gene_name` aside as a
   label map, keep the sample columns. Round the length-scaled estimates to integers (the
   counts DESeq2-family methods expect). Nothing is written back to the input.
3. **Align with the design.** Read `design`; require that its `sample_id` set equals the count
   matrix's sample columns exactly (abort otherwise), and order columns by `sample_id`.
4. **Filter** genes with fewer than 10 total counts across all samples (zero-count genes carry
   no information and destabilise dispersion fitting). Record genes before/after.
5. **Transform.** Variance-stabilising transform with pydeseq2: `DeseqDataSet(counts,
   metadata, design="~condition")`, `fit_size_factors()`, then `vst(use_design=False)` — i.e.
   **blind** to condition, so the transform cannot pull samples toward their group.
6. **Select genes.** The top 500 genes by variance of the VST values — or all filtered genes if
   fewer than 500 remain (the matrix currently has 200 genes, so this will be all of them).
7. **PCA.** `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=min(n_samples, n_genes))` on the
   selected VST matrix (samples as observations; each gene centred, not scaled — the DESeq2
   `plotPCA` convention). Record per-PC explained-variance ratio.
8. **Plot.** PC1 vs PC2 scatter, one point per sample, coloured by `condition`
   (2 levels: untreated, treated), each point labelled with its `sample_id`; axis labels carry
   the percentage of variance explained. Saved as PNG (300 dpi) and PDF.
9. **Write tables**: sample coordinates on every PC (with `condition`, `replicate`), and the
   variance explained per PC.

## Outputs
Types come from the closed vocabulary in _references/artifact_types.md.

| File | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `results/pca_pc1_pc2.png` | `figure` | PC1 vs PC2, points coloured by condition, labelled by sample, % variance on axes |
| `results/pca_pc1_pc2.pdf` | `figure` | the same plot, vector format |
| `results/pca_coordinates.csv` | `table` | `sample_id,condition,replicate,PC1..PCk` — every sample's score on every PC |
| `results/pca_variance_explained.csv` | `table` | `pc,variance_ratio,cumulative_ratio` |

No vocabulary type more specific than `figure`/`table` fits a PCA; the VST matrix is an
intermediate and is not declared.

## Execution
Runs: batch
One job through `_system/executorlib.py submit`, in `gars-bio`: 1 CPU, 4 GB memory, 15 min
time limit. Expected wall time under 2 minutes (6 samples x 200 genes); expected output under
1 MB in `results/`.
